WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:04.140
Ezra Bridger has finally made&nbsp;
his long-awaited return to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:06.960
Star Wars franchise — but did you&nbsp;
recognize the actor who plays him?

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.640
Star Wars fans had a lot to get excited about&nbsp;
going into the Disney+ series Ahsoka. Not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:16.020
was Ahsoka Tano about to get a live-action project&nbsp;
of her own after over a decade in animation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:20.340
but the series was to mark the live-action&nbsp;
debut of beloved Rebels characters. As the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:23.640
series unfolded, the likes of Hera&nbsp;
Syndulla and Sabine Wren took the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.000
spotlight. While it's been great to see&nbsp;
these animated favorites in live-action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.600
fans have continued to ask the big&nbsp;
question: where's Ezra Bridger?

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:32.760
Ezra last appeared in the Rebels finale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.760 --> 00:00:36.420
in which a pod of purrgil take him and Grand&nbsp;
Admiral Thrawn out of the known galaxy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.700
leaving Sabine and the rest of his friends behind.&nbsp;
Years after his disappearance, Sabine travels to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.660
a distant world in the hopes of finding him&nbsp;
— and sure enough, Ezra is alive and well.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:48.320
"I can't wait to go home."

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:52.860
For Ahsoka, actor Eman Esfandi takes over&nbsp;
from Rebels voice actor Taylor Gray. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.580
entirely possible that you've seen Esfandi&nbsp;
before — though it's not all that likely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.780
This is because, with Ahsoka, he is adding a&nbsp;
major credit to his rather short filmography.

00:01:00.780 --> 00:01:04.800
Unlike many of the actors on Ahsoka, who&nbsp;
boast robust acting résumés that go back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.180
decades and span screens big and small,&nbsp;
Eman Esfandi is only just starting out in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:14.340
the industry. His earliest credit is from 2017&nbsp;
in the form of the short film FU Glory Days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.300
in which he takes on the role of Hammer.&nbsp;
His filmography is largely populated by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:23.460
more short films from subsequent years,&nbsp;
such as 2018's Why We Fight and 2019's I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.780
Am Mackenzie, although Esfandi does have some&nbsp;
feature-length efforts to his name as well.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:31.140
Esfandi scored his first feature&nbsp;
film credit in 2018's Red 11,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:35.580
which was directed by none other than Robert&nbsp;
Rodriguez, who also directed episodes of The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:39.960
Mandalorian and Book of Boba Fett. Esfandi&nbsp;
later took roles in Phaedra, Austin Weird,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:44.100
and the Will Smith-led King Richard. Right&nbsp;
now, the most recent silver screen production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:49.020
under Esfandi's belt was 2022's The Inspection —&nbsp;
though it stands to reason that his body of work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:52.680
acting talent, and exposure from Ahsoka will&nbsp;
bring him more roles in the near future.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.580
Notably, Esfandi has done a superb&nbsp;
job filling very big shoes — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:00.360
under difficult circumstances. If there's one&nbsp;
thing the Star Wars fandom has become known&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.720
for over the years, it's a reluctance&nbsp;
and occasional hostility toward change.

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:08.880
“Wow, you’re about as bright&nbsp;
as a binary droid. They’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:11.223
not going to come for me. People don’t do that.”

00:02:11.223 --> 00:02:14.280
So, when the news broke that Eman Esfandi&nbsp;
was on the hook to take the baton from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:18.360
Taylor Gray as Ezra Bridger, some online&nbsp;
fans naturally pushed back. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:21.960
while fans in certain corners of the Internet&nbsp;
voiced their displeasure at this new casting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:25.740
most folks were excited to see where Esfandi's&nbsp;
take on the character was headed. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:29.700
before the series even released, fans expressed&nbsp;
a great deal of support for his casting.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:33.900
Back in December 2022, a Redditor posted&nbsp;
an interview with Esfandi in which he had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:36.840
been asked about his casting as Ezra&nbsp;
in Ahsoka. In the comment section,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.840
Star Wars fans were blown away&nbsp;
by how perfectly cast he was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.560
with his voice standing out as a solid match&nbsp;
for an aged-up version of the character.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.323
“That’s the Idea”

00:02:45.323 --> 00:02:48.720
In another thread, fans were amazed by&nbsp;
how much Esfandi looks like the animated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:52.260
version of Ezra and discussed how they couldn't&nbsp;
wait to see him suit up on the small screen.

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:57.300
So far, both Esfandi and the live-action version&nbsp;
of Ezra appear to be a hit. And we'll no doubt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:00.720
see more of this version of the character&nbsp;
as Ahsoka's first season draws to a close.

